        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "saek",
          "text": "In Denver, a T-bone accident—also called a side-impact collision—happens when one vehicle crashes into the side of another, often at intersections. These accidents can lead to serious injuries and costly financial damages. Understanding your legal rights is crucial if you are involved in a T-bone crash. Insurance companies may attempt to minimize your claim, but a skilled ",
          "marks": []
        },
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "saem",
          "text": "car accident lawyer in Denver",
          "marks": [
            "lael"
          ]
        },
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "saen",
          "text": " can protect your interests and help you pursue the compensation you deserve.",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_type": "block",
      "_key": "baer",
      "style": "h2",
      "markDefs": [],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "saeq",
          "text": "What Is a T-Bone Accident?",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_type": "block",
      "_key": "baet",
      "style": "normal",
      "markDefs": [],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "saes",
          "text": "A T-bone (also called a broadside or right-angle collision) accident occurs when the front of one vehicle collides with the driver or passenger side of another car, making the two vehicles form a “T” shape. These accidents are most common at intersections in Denver, and can occur if one driver runs a red light or fails to yield the right-of-way. They can cause catastrophic damage, particularly to the vehicle struck, as the sides of a car are not designed to absorb the force of an impact as well as the front or rear.",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_type": "block",
      "_key": "baez",
      "style": "normal",
      "markDefs": [],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "saeu",
          "text": "As reported by Denver’s KDVR, the Colorado State Patrol data shows that Denver drivers are running more stop signs and red lights than ever. According to the same reporting and data, Colorado ranks 6th highest in the nation for fatal crashes resulting from running red lights.",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_type": "block",
      "_key": "baf2",
      "style": "h2",
      "markDefs": [],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "saf1",
          "text": "Who Is Liable for a T-Bone Accident in Denver?",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_type": "block",
      "_key": "baf4",
      "style": "normal",
      "markDefs": [],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "saf3",
          "text": "Colorado has a fault-based car insurance system. This means that after an automobile accident, victims can file claims against the driver or party at fault for causing the collision. In a no-fault state, on the other hand, all drivers seek compensation from their own car insurance companies, regardless of fault. Colorado switched from a no-fault state to a fault state on July 1, 2003, when the Colorado legislature allowed the no-fault statute to sunset.",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_type": "block",
      "_key": "baf9",
      "style": "normal",
      "markDefs": [
        {
          "_key": "laf6",
          "_type": "link",
          "href": "/determining-fault-after-a-car-accident/"
        }
      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "saf5",
          "text": "This means that since July 1, 2003, e fault law means that the cause of a T-bone collision ",
          "marks": []
        },
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "saf7",
          "text": "must be determined",
          "marks": [
            "laf6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "saf8",
          "text": " before an injured victim can file an insurance claim. Determining liability, or legal responsibility, for a side-impact crash typically takes an investigation into which of the drivers was in the wrong place at the wrong time; in other words, which driver was in violation of a traffic law at the time of the crash. Sometimes, these investigations turn up results that are unfair or incorrect, and if you are injured in such a crash but have been wrongly determined to be at fault, a personal injury trial lawyer can help overturn that liability determination and then pursue a claim for your injuries.",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_type": "block",
      "_key": "bafc",
      "style": "h2",
      "markDefs": [],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "safb",
          "text": "Common Causes of Denver T-Bone Accidents",
